# vehje-fixpoint. backlog

Designed but deferred. Names stay unbackticked until they ship.

## The full multi-relation join

The shipping engine evaluates the reach and effect fixpoints (single-body and
small-arity rules) with whole-column and semi-naive evaluation. A full
worst-case-optimal multi-relation join (leapfrog-triejoin, with AGM bounds) is
what a rich multi-way rule set wants and is the next lever if a client's rules
need it; the reach and effect inference do not.

## Differential retraction

The incremental layer ships the additive-edit case (an edit adds and re-derives
transitive dependents). General edit with retraction (a deletion that must
un-derive what depended on it) needs counted differential dataflow. Defer until
a consumer's edit pattern needs retraction rather than rebuild-the-affected-set.

## Upstream promotion

The engine is kept generic and vehje-ir-free partly so its semi-naive core can
be promoted upstream into the stack if a second consumer outside vehje
materialises. Today the congruence closure and the provenance-semiring grade
plumbing are vehje-shaped, so it stays here; revisit if another stack consumer
wants a bare semi-naive engine.

## Deepdive owed

A deepdive on the relational engine (semi-naive evaluation, congruence, the
incremental scaling mechanism, and the bench evidence: 8M nodes and 16M edges in
about 50 ms, the 2000-mod incremental numbers) lands with the first real client.
